    "answer": {
      "short": "No finite entry fee gives the casino an expected profit, because the expected payout is infinite.",
      "worked": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "a65b9066-6e59-4cdf-936f-67b4b6066275",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 0,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Let ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "X",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " be the payout in dollars. The expected value is",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "break"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\begin{align*}E(X)&=2\\left(\\frac12\\right)+4\\left(\\frac14\\right)+8\\left(\\frac18\\right)+16\\left(\\frac1{16}\\right)+\\cdots\\\\E(X)&=1+1+1+1+\\cdots\\\\E(X)&=\\infty.\\end{align*}",
                "display": true
              },
              {
                "type": "break"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The expected payout is infinite, so there is no finite amount the casino can charge to make an expected profit under this unlimited-jackpot model. Patrons may nevertheless be unwilling to pay a large entry fee because the later, larger payouts have very low probabilities.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
